00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:04.230
Rice is one of the world's oldest crops.
The first accounts of rice farming date

2
00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:09.730
back to China in 2800 BC. It has since
become the world's second highest

3
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:13.740
produced food, with over 40 thousand
different varieties in cultivation.

4
00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:19.000
Most of the world's rice is produced
and consumed in Eastern Asia. In 2012,

5
00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:25.320
for example, over 400 thousand tons of
rice were consumed. Rice is a

6
00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:29.080
grass-like plant that is grown in
semi-flooded fields. The water acts

7
00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.619
like a protective blanket, which helps
the plants survive temperature changes

8
00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:37.280
and prevents the growth of weeds.
However, if a bad storm comes through

9
00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:42.190
and floods the plains completely, well,
after a few days of being underwater,

10
00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:44.150
the rice will die.

11
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.090
Well these are not the best of times
for some farmers in Kwara state, with

12
00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:55.430
at least 458 hectares of rice farms now
lying underwater. Myanmar is the

13
00:00:55.430 --> 00:01:00.780
world's sixth largest rice exporter,
but after weeks of devastating floods,

14
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:06.070
the country is facing food shortages
and has suspended shipments abroad. As

15
00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:10.520
the world's climate warms, storms that
bring destructive flooding are becoming

16
00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:14.630
increasingly stronger and more
frequent. Over time, this could bring

17
00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:19.160
massive food shortages to parts of Asia
that heavily depend on this crop. As

18
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:24.050
people clamor to find a way to keep the
rice alive, science may just have the

19
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.140
answer we're looking for.

